Oszillator-Prüfstand / Oscillator Test Stand

Description
An Arduino-based test stand for measuring the frequency stability of a simple pendulum – inspired by balance wheel regulation in mechanical watches.

Components

1x Small breadboard
1x Arduino Uno R3
1x RGB LED (on pins 8, 9, and 10)
1x CNY70 sensor (optical reflective sensor, on pin A2)
1x 10 kΩ resistor
5x 220 Ω resistors

Circuit Diagram / Wiring Diagram
***Important note:***
The circuit diagram and wiring diagram are in the additional document (PDF) in Section 3. There you will find the plans and important additional notes to understand the circuit diagram.

In the simulation (Tinkercad), a push button was used instead of the CNY70 because the sensor is not available there. The position of the push button and the wiring in the simulation correspond exactly to the connection of the real sensor.

How It Works
The pendulum swings and interrupts the light beam of the CNY70 sensor. The Arduino measures the time between interruptions and calculates the frequency from that.

Initial state: The RGB LED lights up red.
On each detection: Every time the pendulum passes the sensor, the LED blinks green.
After 5 stable periods: If no anomaly is detected in 5 consecutive measurements, the LED lights up green continuously.
In case of an anomaly: If the frequency is outside the allowed range, the system stops, the LED lights up blue, and an error message is displayed.

Acceptable frequency range:

Reference frequency: ~0.85 Hz
Upper limit: 1.0 Hz
Lower limit: 0.75 Hz
Maximum deviation: ~12–18%

Execution

Build the circuit according to the circuit diagram.
Open the code in the Arduino IDE and upload it to the board.
Open the serial monitor (9600 baud) to see the measured frequency.
Set the pendulum in motion and observe the LED indicator.

Results

Measured frequency: ~0.85 Hz
Deviation: ~12–18% (within the acceptable range)

